<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Herodian Kingdom of Judea</h1><p class="st-meta">37 BCE – 4 BCE · capital Jerusalem</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of Herod the Great, a Roman client state that rebuilt Jerusalem's Temple and left fortresses like Masada.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Herod, an Idumaean noble whose father had served Hasmonean rulers, was named king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judea</a> by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-rome">Roman Senate</a> in 40 BCE. With Roman help he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-i-monophthalmus">Antigonus</a>, last of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hasmonean-kingdom">Hasmonean Kingdom</a>, in 37 BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Herod ruled as a loyal client of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-antony">Mark Antony</a> and then Augustus. He rebuilt the Temple on a vast platform, founded the harbour city of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-caesarea">Caesarea</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/laanemaa">Maritima</a> and built palaces at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masada">Masada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodium">Herodium</a>. His rule was harsh and suspicious; he executed his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariamne-i">Mariamne</a> and several of his sons.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Herod's death in 4 BCE, Augustus divided the kingdom among his sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-archelaus">Archelaus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-antipas">Herod Antipas</a> and Philip. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaea">Judea</a> itself became a Roman province in 6 CE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/herodian-kingdom-of-judea.jpg" alt="Herodian Kingdom of Judea" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: DEGA MD · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herodian_Kingdom_topographic_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>37 BCE – 4 BCE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jerusalem</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Herod the Great</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Judaism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aramaic, Greek, Hebrew</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>, <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>, <a href="/countries/jordan">Jordan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Herodian Kingdom, Kingdom of Herod</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-20&amp;focus=herodian-kingdom-of-judea" aria-label="Herodian Kingdom of Judea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5588 1021 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Herodian Kingdom of Judea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5908 1277L5908 1277M5908 1277L5304 942M5908 1277L5304 942M5908 1277L5908 1277M5908 1277L5911 1242M5908 1277L5913 1227"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5908 1277h0M5304 942h0M5304 942h0M5908 1277h0M5911 1242h0M5913 1227h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5908 1277h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5908 1277h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jerusalem. <a href="/atlas?year=-20&amp;focus=herodian-kingdom-of-judea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
